       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not going to enter this order as written. 5,100 mg/day of metformin is about twice the maximum labeled dose. Doses above about 2,550 mg/day add no glycemic benefit, but they do raise the risk of lactic acidosis and severe GI effects. You've said it's intentional but haven't given a clinical reason, so I can't treat it as anything other than a probable error. The harm could be serious and hard to reverse.\n\nI can enter any of these now:\n- **Metformin 850 mg PO three times daily with meals.** This is 2,550 mg/day, the maximum for immediate-release.\n- **A lower dose or a different schedule.** Tell me what you'd like.\n- **A different agent, if you need more glucose control than the maximum metformin dose gives.** I can enter an add-on, such as a basal insulin or another oral agent, if you give me the drug and dose.\n\nIf you have a specific protocol or indication that calls for this dose, tell me what it is. You can also place the order directly in the order-entry system and go through pharmacy verification. I just don't want to be the one to enter it as written."
      }
